# nl-config-2: the usage history of the earlier daemon is imported into the statistics (mixdog-ledger `history`).

## native/core/crates/mixdog-ledger/Cargo.toml

ALREADY APPLIED by t1-config-2 (the crate's own file): [dependencies] gained tokio-postgres (no dev feature change). Original text of the wire follows.

[dependencies]: add (the database reader of the import; it drives its own connection, so no tokio edge is needed):

    tokio-postgres = { workspace = true }

## native/core/Cargo.lock

Package `mixdog-ledger`: add `"tokio-postgres"` to its dependencies (it is already in the lock, used by mixdog-services).

## native/core/crates/mixdog-workspace-hack/Cargo.toml

Nothing by hand: tokio-postgres with `with-serde_json-1` is already unified through mixdog-services. Run the hakari check
and take its output if it asks for a line.

## native/core/crates/mixdog-core-base/src/pricing/ledger_pricer.rs (CatalogPricer)

`Pricer` (mixdog-ledger refresh.rs) gained a provided method `price_aggregate(&self, request: &PriceRequest) -> Result<Priced>`
whose default is `self.price(request)`. The restored legacy days of the import are priced as daily sums, which the catalog
does with `UsageArgs { historical_aggregate: true, .. }` (a route with context-size tiers is unpriced: a sum cannot say which
requests crossed one). In `impl Pricer for CatalogPricer` add an override that builds the same `UsageArgs` as `price` with
`historical_aggregate: true`, for example by moving the body of `price` into a private `fn priced(&self, request: &PriceRequest,
aggregate: bool)` and having `price` call it with `false` and `price_aggregate` with `true`. Without it a restored day with a
tiered route is priced as one request. Add a test next to `a_cold_catalog_prices_and_stamps_as_nodes_does`: a route whose
metadata has `pricingTiers` with `aboveInputTokens` below the sums is unpriced through `price_aggregate` and priced through `price`.

## native/core/crates/mixdog-core/src/run_host/ledger_source.rs (LedgerSource::open)

The history import is off until it is enabled on the ledger's refresher. In `fn open`, right after
`let refresher = UsageRefresher::new(self.pricer.clone(), self.lanes.cpu_bulk);` add:

    refresher.enable_history(self.plugin_data.clone(), self.lanes.maintenance_io);

(`plugin_data` is `resolvePluginData()`, the directory the importer reads `history/agent-trace.jsonl*`,
`gateway-usage.local.jsonl|json`, `usage-rollup.local.json` and `pgdata/postmaster.pid` from, as Node passed it.) Every
`UsageLedger::get_usage_stats` then imports once before its read. Opening a ledger in a test that names no data directory stays
as it is: a refresher without `enable_history` imports nothing.

## SERVICE_PENDING (compose_flip.rs)

No name to remove: this task registers no service and adds no protocol method.

## Later tasks

None. The `usage-rollup.local.json` family is retired by the migration only after the import marked the ledger
(`metadata.importedThrough`); `mixdog-migrate` `stores.rs` retains them until then and says so.


Corrective source-oracle follow-up (2026-11-15)
---------------------------------------------
Owned implementation changes:
- HistoryImporter::ensure and UsageRefresher::import_history now return Result<()>.
  UsageLedger::get_usage_stats propagates it before refresh/read.
- LedgerError::SharedHistory(Arc<LedgerError>) preserves the exact shared failure.
  LedgerError::unshared() returns the underlying typed error; History(String)
  displays the original message without an added history prefix.
- Root consumer: mixdog-core-domains/src/domains/ai_account/failure.rs,
  ledger_failure: change `match error` to `match error.unshared()` so Busy and
  Stopped remain QueueFull and Cancelled through shared history failures.
  Existing message/queue patterns bind references; use the same displayed text.
  Exact body of `ledger_failure` after the change:

      match error.unshared() {
          LedgerError::Period { message, .. } => failed(domain, *message),
          LedgerError::Busy { queue } => CoreError::QueueFull { queue: (*queue).into() },
          LedgerError::Stopped => CoreError::Cancelled { by: "caller".into() },
          other => failed(domain, other.to_string()),
      }
- UsageWriter::import_history uses enqueue_write and always settles the accepted
  mark on success or failure. No change to its Result<HistoryOutcome> consumer.
- Original file/database/gateway batches commit in original order. Each original
  accept(rows) is one transaction, not separate 512-row commits. Live append
  batching remains unchanged. Large history files can occupy the writer longer.

Recorded oracle and strict tests:
- src/history/oracle/record.mjs runs actual original Node 22.20.0 import, pricing,
  SQLite ledger and usage-stats-api, with owned temporary files and :memory: DB.
  readTrace is injected; no PostgreSQL connection or real profile is accessed.
  Its load hook records actual priceUsage arguments/results, preserving its body.
  The synthetic catalog contents and original source SHA-256 hashes are embedded.
- src/history/oracle/original.json: 17 file/trace/rollup scenarios plus 2 API cases.
  No network attempts. Actual flat and tiered historical aggregate pricing,
  local/unknown routes, 1h cache writes, provider-reported cost, partial commits,
  malformed input errors, torn lines, gateway primitives/null, cutover and repeat.
- src/history/original_twin.rs is included from existing history/tests.rs.
  Tests: original_node_history_errors_partial_commits_pricing_and_receipts;
  concurrent_import_waiters_share_failure_without_retry_and_later_call_retries;
  rejected_history_write_settles_its_accepted_mark;
  original_queued_live_write_precedes_statistics_cutover.
- Run with TZ=UTC (oracle's explicit calendar zone). No tolerance/skip gates.
  Tests compare exact stored events, route signatures/rates, legacy documents,
  completion metadata, receipts, errors, shared failure identity and write marks.
- IMPORTANT: RecordedPrices is an oracle boundary, NOT a CatalogPricer parity
  claim. Root must also replay pricingCalls through its actual CatalogPricer,
  including price_aggregate historicalAggregate=true (original wiring above).
- Required existing dependency: tokio-postgres as above. The concurrency test
  arms its limit through mixdog_rt::time::timeout, so no tokio feature is added.
  No other dependencies or registration changes.

Verification limits:
- Recorder executed; Rust build/tests intentionally not run in this worker.
- Original SQLite worker/file-backed recordQueued path is not executed by the
  memory-only oracle; its queue barrier needs root integration regression.
- Native OS read-error wording and live PostgreSQL client error behavior are
  not certified by isolated fixtures. No real service was contacted.
- Nonstandard malformed legacy bucket scalar/object coercion is not covered by
  these representative fixtures; do not infer complete arbitrary-JSON parity.
